OPTIONAL QEMU patch "hyperv-wake" for QEMU 10.2.4 (hw/i386/pc.c, function pc_machine_wakeup). kayfabe does not need it to run.

  Apply by hand:   patch -p1 -d <qemu-10.2.4 source tree> < hyperv-wake.patch
  Apply with kit:  python3 -I scripts/release/build_qemu.py build --with-patch hyperv-wake ...   (default: not applied)
  Bench build:     python3 -I scripts/bench/build_kf3_patched.py --with-patch hyperv-wake <qemu-tree>   (builds a copy of the tree)

What it fixes. QEMU's ACPI S3 wake (system_wakeup, pc_machine_wakeup) resets every vCPU with a cold CPU reset. That zeroes
every Hyper-V synthetic MSR field of CPUX86State (they sit before end_reset_fields) and writes the zeros to KVM, among them
HV_X64_MSR_REFERENCE_TSC, while the guest TSC restarts near 0. A Windows guest does not re-enable its Hyper-V TSC page on
resume, keeps reading the stale page, its reference time goes negative, and the one-shot synthetic-timer deadlines it writes
make KVM re-arm a timer that never fires: all vCPUs wait in HLT forever. The patch saves the Hyper-V MSR block of every vCPU
before the reset and puts it back after it, so KVM republishes a valid page for the restarted TSC. Architectural CPU state
is reset as before; a guest without hv-* flags carries zeros through it, so the patch changes nothing for it.

Who needs it. Only a guest that has the Hyper-V enlightenments (hv-time with hv-synic and hv-stimer, as kayfabe's Windows
gate configuration passes them) and does ACPI S3 sleep and wake on a host whose TSC is stable for KVM (a valid guest TSC
page). A host with an unstable TSC, a guest without the enlightenments, and a Linux guest do not need it.

Evidence (kayfabe repository): docs/design/V3_S3_RESUME.md section 7b and traces/win11_s3_wake_20261123/README.md. Measured
there: a stable-TSC host, the enlightenment flags of the gate configuration, stock QEMU 0 of 1 wakes clean, with this patch
3 of 3. Without the enlightenments (the documented `host,-vmx` flags) stock QEMU was clean for one cycle, only one was run.

Upstream. Read on 2026-11-24: QEMU 10.2.4 and master have the same pc_machine_wakeup (no Hyper-V state is kept), and
docs/system/i386/hyperv.rst says nothing about reset or sleep. No QEMU issue or list thread for this exact symptom was found.
Related, none of them fixes it:
  https://gitlab.com/qemu-project/qemu/-/issues/1152
      Windows crash after system_wakeup with hv-tlbflush (a different symptom, open).
  https://lkml.iu.edu/2601.1/09282.html  and  https://lkml.iu.edu/2601.3/00359.html
      KVM "Delay firing of expired stimers" (Alexander Graf, January 2026), for Windows Server 2025 hibernation; its author
      put it on hold on 2026-01-24 and no merge was found, so a host kernel cannot be assumed to have it.
  https://lkml.iu.edu/2607.0/07481.html  and  https://patchew.org/linux/20260714133212.3916611-3-clopez@suse.de/
      KVM "Clamp stimer deadline to avoid livelock" (Carlos Lopez, July 2026, kvm-x86 commit 0ca49fbd2883, Cc stable). It
      saturates an overflowing deadline; inferred, not run: it would end the re-arm spin but the timer still never fires.
This patch is not proposed upstream: it is a workaround in the machine's wake path, and a fix may belong in KVM (the page)
or in how the reset treats the Hyper-V state.

The hunk is a plain unified diff against the pristine 10.2.4 file; the kit applies it only to text that matches exactly.

diff --git a/hw/i386/pc.c b/hw/i386/pc.c
--- a/hw/i386/pc.c
+++ b/hw/i386/pc.c
@@ -1720,10 +1720,33 @@
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * A guest S3 wake keeps the Hyper-V synthetic MSR state of every vCPU. The wake's reset zeroes it (it sits before
+ * end_reset_fields) and writes it to KVM while the guest TSC restarts, so a guest with hv-time keeps reading a stale
+ * reference TSC page.
+ */
+#define KF_HV_LO offsetof(CPUX86State, msr_hv_hypercall)
+#define KF_HV_LEN (offsetof(CPUX86State, msr_hv_tsc_emulation_status) + sizeof(uint64_t) - KF_HV_LO)
 static void pc_machine_wakeup(MachineState *machine)
 {
+    CPUState *cs;
+    size_t n = 0, i = 0;
+    uint8_t *save;
+
     cpu_synchronize_all_states();
+    CPU_FOREACH(cs) {
+        n++;
+    }
+    save = g_malloc(n * KF_HV_LEN);
+    CPU_FOREACH(cs) {
+        memcpy(save + KF_HV_LEN * i++, (uint8_t *)&X86_CPU(cs)->env + KF_HV_LO, KF_HV_LEN);
+    }
     pc_machine_reset(machine, RESET_TYPE_WAKEUP);
+    i = 0;
+    CPU_FOREACH(cs) {
+        memcpy((uint8_t *)&X86_CPU(cs)->env + KF_HV_LO, save + KF_HV_LEN * i++, KF_HV_LEN);
+    }
+    g_free(save);
     cpu_synchronize_all_post_reset();
 }
 
